<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">5th Dalai Lama</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1617">1617</a>–<a href="/years/1682">1682</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Fifth Dalai Lama, Ngawang Lobsang Gyatso, Great Fifth</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The 5th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a>, Ngawang Lobsang Gyatso, was the first Dalai Lama to hold political power over all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. In 1642 his patron <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gushi-khan">Güshi Khan</a> of the Khoshut <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> defeated the Tsangpa kings and handed him authority, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganden-phodrang">Ganden Phodrang</a> government. Known as the Great Fifth, he began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potala-palace">Potala Palace</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>, strengthened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> school and visited the Qing court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in 1652–1653.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama.jpg" alt="5th Dalai Lama" width="320" height="520" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thangka_of_the_5th_Dalai_Lama.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>He was born in late 1617 as Künga Migyur in the Chonggye Valley in Ü, about two days' journey south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>, into the aristocratic Zahor family, whose seat since the 14th century was Taktsé Castle. His father, Dudul Rabten, was arrested in 1618 for taking part in a plot against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karma-phuntsok-namgyal">Karma Phuntsok Namgyal</a>, leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsang">Tsang</a> hegemony. The child's name was drawn by lot from three candidates in divination rituals held in secret at Radeng monastery, because the king of Tsang had forbidden the search for the 4th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a>'s reincarnation. Sonam Choephel, the 4th Dalai Lama's chief attendant, is credited with finding him.</p><p>He received the name Lobsang Gyatso when he took novice ordination at Drepung, and Ngawang was added at his full ordination at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jokhang">Jokhang Temple</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> in 1638. Though a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> scholar, he also studied Nyingma doctrines and took Nyingma tantric empowerments.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Sonam Choephel, regent in his youth, asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gushi-khan">Güshi Khan</a>, head of the Khoshut, to act militarily in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a>'s name. Güshi Khan conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kham">Kham</a> in 1640, and his victory over Karma Tenkyong, prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsang">Tsang</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shigatse">Shigatse</a> in 1642 completed the conquest and established the Khoshut Khanate. The Dalai Lama was then invested with temporal power over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. He again proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> the capital, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a> had, appointed governors and ministers, promulgated laws, and made his regent a prime minister, the Desi.</p><p>He began building the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potala-palace">Potala Palace</a> in 1645 on a site between Drepung and Sera monasteries, and moved into the White Palace in 1649; the Red Palace was added in 1690–1694. He was the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a> to accept an invitation from a Chinese emperor to visit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>. At his request the Panchen Lama Lobsang Chökyi Gyaltsen, his tutor, accepted Tashilhunpo Monastery as his seat for future incarnations. In 1679 he launched an expedition against his Prime Minister's advice, leading to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet-ladakh-mughal-war">Tibet–Ladakh–Mughal War</a>, which ended in 1684 with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tingmosgang">Treaty of Tingmosgang</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died in 1682 at the age of 65. His death was concealed by his Prime Minister, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangye-gyatso">Desi Sangye Gyatso</a>, so that the Potala could be finished and to prevent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>'s neighbours from exploiting a gap in the succession. He wrote 24 volumes of scholarly and religious works, among them a detailed history of Tibet written in 1643 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gushi-khan">Güshi Khan</a>'s request. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> call him the Great Fifth.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “5th Dalai Lama” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After his death in 1653 his son Sengge succeeded him but was murdered in 1670, and Sengge's brother Galdan, who had studied as a Buddhist monk in Tibet, returned to take power, defeated his rivals and received the title Boshugtu Khan from the <b>Fifth Dalai Lama</b> around 1678.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Fifth Dalai Lama</b> converted some Bon monasteries to the Gelug school by force, yet officially recognised Bon as a Tibetan religion, and Samten Karmay sees his choice of a Dru son as Panchen Lama as a gesture of reconciliation.</blockquote><a href="/religion/bon">Bon · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1642 the Oirat ruler Güshi Khan conferred authority over Tibet on the <b>Fifth Dalai Lama</b>, whose Ganden Phodrang government ruled from Lhasa under Mongol and, from 1720, Qing protection.</blockquote><a href="/government/theocracy">Theocracy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Ngawang Lozang Gyatso, the <b>Great Fifth</b> Dalai Lama, started building the present palace in 1645.</blockquote><a href="/work/potala-palace">Potala Palace · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1642 Güshi Khan of the Khoshut Khanate defeated the Tsangpa dynasty and conferred authority over Tibet on the <b>Fifth Dalai Lama</b>, Ngawang Lobsang Gyatso.</blockquote><a href="/state/ganden-phodrang">Ganden Phodrang</a></li><li><blockquote>Güshi Khan took the title King of Tibet but handed civil and religious authority to the <b>Fifth Dalai Lama</b> in the Ganden Phodrang, keeping military power and protecting the Gelug.</blockquote><a href="/state/khoshut-khanate">Khoshut Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=29.65,91.1&amp;year=1659" aria-label="Tibet on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of 5th Dalai Lama"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5082 5030h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5082 5030h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=29.65,91.1&amp;year=1659">What was here in 1659? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
